      "step": 2,
      "phase": "Leaves and Outcomes",
      "content": "Each leaf node represents a unique permutation of the input elements that is considered sorted. For n distinct elements, there are n! (n factorial) possible permutations."
    },
    {
      "step": 3,
      "phase": "Tree Height vs. Leaves",
      "content": "If a binary tree has height h (the number of comparisons in the worst case), the maximum number of leaves it can have is 2^h. Therefore, 2^h >= n!."
    },
    {
      "step": 4,
      "phase": "Mathematical Derivation",
      "content": "Take log base 2 of both sides: h >= log(n!). Using Stirling's approximation, log(n!) ≈ n log n - O(n). The dominant term is n log n."
    },
    {
      "step": 5,
      "phase": "Conclusion",
      "content": "The height of the decision tree represents the minimum number of comparisons T(n). Since T(n) >= log(n!) = Omega(n log n), no comparison-based sorting algorithm can perform better than this bound in the worst case."
    }
