  "correct_option": "C",
  "option_text": "CO2 concentration",
  "detailed_explanation": "C4 plants possess a specialized structural arrangement (Kranz anatomy) that spatially separates initial carbon fixation from the Calvin cycle. This allows C4 plants to concentrate CO2 in the bundle-sheath cells. This elevated CO2 concentration suppresses the oxygenase activity of Rubisco (preventing photorespiration), whereas C3 plants are limited by lower CO2 concentrations which allow Rubisco to bind oxygen instead. While temperature and water availability affect photosynthesis in both groups, the primary inherent factor determining the efficiency differential between the two pathways is the management of CO2 concentration."
